    "title": "pandas.api.types.is_categorical_dtype — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1386.gcb2086a1a4 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.api.types.is_categorical_dtype#\n\n- \npandas.api.types.is_categorical_dtype(*arr_or_dtype* )[source]#\n- Check whether an array-like or dtype is of the Categorical dtype. Deprecated since version 2.2.0: Use isinstance(dtype, pd.CategoricalDtype) instead. \n  - Parameters:\n    - **arr_or_dtype** array-like or dtype\n    - The array-like or dtype to check.\n  - Returns:\n    - boolean\n    - Whether or not the array-like or dtype is of the Categorical dtype.\n See also \n  - `api.types.is_list_like`\n  - Check if the object is list-like.\n  - `api.types.is_complex_dtype`\n  - Check whether the provided array or dtype is of a complex dtype.\n Examples >>> from pandas.api.types import is_categorical_dtype >>> from pandas import CategoricalDtype >>> is_categorical_dtype(object) False >>> is_categorical_dtype(CategoricalDtype()) True >>> is_categorical_dtype([1, 2, 3]) False >>> is_categorical_dtype(pd.Categorical([1, 2, 3])) True >>> is_categorical_dtype(pd.CategoricalIndex([1, 2, 3])) True",
